.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt IOEVENT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ioevent
.Nd submit and reap asynchronous I/O requests on a completion port
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ioport.h
.Ft int
.Fn ioevent "int pfd" "const struct io_submit *submits" "int nsubmits" "struct io_completion *completions" "int ncompletions" "const struct timespec *timeout"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn ioevent
system call submits a batch of asynchronous requests to the completion port
identified by
.Fa pfd
.Pq see Xr ioport 2 ,
and/or reaps a batch of their completions.
Its shape mirrors
.Xr kevent 2 :
one call may submit up to
.Fa nsubmits
requests from the array
.Fa submits ,
reap up to
.Fa ncompletions
completions into the array
.Fa completions ,
and, when no completion is immediately available, wait as directed by
.Fa timeout .
.Pp
If
.Fa ncompletions
is zero, the call only submits and returns immediately.
If
.Fa nsubmits
is zero, the call only reaps and, if necessary, waits.
.Ss Submission records
Each element of
.Fa submits
is a
.Vt struct io_submit :
.Bd -literal
struct io_submit {
    uint64_t tag;        /* caller tag, echoed in the completion */
    uint32_t opcode;     /* IO_* or IO_CANCEL */
    int32_t  fd;         /* target fd, or -1 */
    uint32_t flags;      /* IOSUB_* */
    uint32_t reserved0;  /* must be zero */
    union {
        struct { void *buf; uint64_t len; uint64_t offset; } rw;
        struct { struct iovec *iov; uint64_t iovcnt;
                 uint64_t offset; } rwv;
        struct { uint64_t ident; int32_t filter; uint32_t fflags;
                 int64_t data; void *udata; } kevent;
        struct { const char *path; uint32_t flags; uint32_t mode;
                 int32_t dirfd; uint32_t pad0; } openat;
    } args;
    uint64_t reserved1[2]; /* must be zero */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Va tag
is an opaque caller value echoed back in the completion.
.Va flags
is reserved; only
.Dv IOSUB_NONE
(0) is currently defined, and
.Va reserved0
and
.Va reserved1
must be zero.
The
.Va args
member is interpreted according to
.Va opcode .
.Ss Opcodes
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv IO_FTRUNCATE"
.It Dv IO_READ
Read
.Va args.rw.len
bytes from
.Va fd
at
.Va args.rw.offset
into the user buffer
.Va args.rw.buf .
.It Dv IO_WRITE
Write
.Va args.rw.len
bytes from the user buffer
.Va args.rw.buf
to
.Va fd
at
.Va args.rw.offset .
.It Dv IO_KEVENT
Wait for the kqueue filter described by
.Va args.kevent
.Pq ident , filter , fflags , data
and, when it fires, complete with the event information.
Currently the
.Ev EVFILT_READ
and
.Ev EVFILT_WRITE
filters on a file descriptor are supported.
.It Dv IO_CLOSE
Close
.Va fd
.Pq equivalent to Xr close 2 ;
.Va args
is unused.
.It Dv IO_CANCEL
A control marker, not a request: it allocates no request, occupies no
completion slot, and produces no completion.
Its only parameter is the caller
.Va tag
of the request to cancel; see
.Sx Cancellation .
.It Dv IO_READV , Dv IO_WRITEV , Dv IO_FSYNC , Dv IO_FTRUNCATE , Dv IO_OPENAT
Reserved opcodes that are not yet implemented.
Submitting one fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
as a direct error return, not as a completion.
.El
.Ss Completion records
Each accepted request produces exactly one completion:
.Bd -literal
struct io_completion {
    uint64_t tag;      /* echoed caller tag */
    int32_t  opcode;   /* IO_* */
    int32_t  error;    /* errno, 0 = success */
    int64_t  result;   /* bytes, new fd, or 0 */
    uint32_t flags;    /* IOCOMPL_* */
    uint32_t reserved; /* must be zero */
};
.Ed
.Pp
On success
.Va error
is zero and
.Va result
is the operation result: the number of bytes transferred for I/O, the new
file descriptor for fd-creating operations, or zero for operations without a
return value.
On failure
.Va error
holds the errno value and
.Va result
is zero.
.Va flags
is reserved; only
.Dv IOCOMPL_NONE
(0) is currently defined, and
.Dv IOCOMPL_MORE
is reserved for future use.
.Ss Acceptance and error channels
The two error channels are distinct:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Synchronous validation failures are returned directly as the result of
.Fn ioevent
and produce no completion.
These include an unknown or unimplemented
.Va opcode
.Pq Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
an invalid
.Va fd
for an fd-based opcode
.Pq Er EBADF ,
a bad
.Va submits ,
.Va completions ,
or
.Va timeout
pointer, and a bad write buffer
.Pq all Er EFAULT ,
and resource exhaustion
.Pq Er ENOMEM .
.It
A request that the port is unable to service asynchronously because it needs
a worker but the port was created with
.Fa nworkers
equal to zero
.Pq a pure asynchronous port, see Xr ioport 2
is still accepted: it completes with
.Va error
set to
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and
.Fn ioevent
returns the number of completions reaped.
.El
.Ss Cancellation
.Dv IO_CANCEL
is a best-effort, silent control marker placed in the
.Fa submits
array.
It targets the in-flight request whose
.Va tag
matches the
.Va tag
field of the
.Dv IO_CANCEL
record.
If no request matches, or the request has already started completing,
.Dv IO_CANCEL
has no effect and reports nothing: it never returns
.Er ENOENT ,
.Er EALREADY ,
or
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A request that has already begun and whose underlying backend cannot be
aborted is not forged as cancelled; it still completes with its real result.
Duplicate tags are legal, but then cancel may hit any one matching request.
Closing the port is the only operation that cancels everything; see
.Xr ioport 2 .
.Ss Waiting
When no completion is available, the behavior is controlled by
.Fa timeout :
.Bl -tag -width ".Fa timeout"
.It Fa timeout No is Dv NULL
Block indefinitely until a completion arrives or a signal is delivered.
.It Fa timeout No is zero Pq Dv { 0, 0 }
Do not block; return immediately.
.It Fa timeout No is non-zero
Block for at most the given interval.
If the interval expires before a completion arrives,
.Fn ioevent
fails with
.Er EWOULDBLOCK .
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn ioevent
returns the number of completions reaped, in the range 0 through
.Fa ncompletions .
Otherwise, -1 is returned and
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn ioevent
system call will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa pfd
is not a valid descriptor, is not a completion port, or the port is being
closed.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa submits ,
.Fa completions ,
or
.Fa timeout
points outside the process address space, or a write buffer could not be
copied in.
.It Bq Er EINTR
The call was interrupted by a signal while waiting.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to accept a request.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa opcode
is unknown or not yet implemented.
.It Bq Er EWOULDBLOCK
The
.Fa timeout
interval expired before any completion arrived.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr ioport 2 ,
.Xr kevent 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr liburing 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn ioevent
system call first appeared in
.Dx 6.5 .
